WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
The seeds that were left are the leading suspect.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.280
But nobody can rerun 1977.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.900
So how do you test what did the selecting?

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.500
Go to the mountain streams of Trinidad, where guppies live.

5
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.380
In some stream reaches, the main predators eat adult guppies.

6
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.000
In others, the main fish predator mostly takes small juveniles.

7
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.410
Guppy populations from the two kinds of site differ in life history.

8
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.590
That means when they mature, at what size, and how many young they produce.

9
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:41.033
So did the predators shape those traits?

10
00:00:42.253 --> 00:00:43.773
The pattern fits that idea.

11
00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:49.973
But a comparison across natural sites shows only an association, not a cause.

12
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.097
The sites differ in other ways too.

13
00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:58.877
Stream size, food, light and history all change from one site to the next.

14
00:01:00.157 --> 00:01:02.257
Comparing the sites cannot pull those apart.

15
00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:05.737
So change the proposed cause on purpose.

16
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:11.557
Guppies from a site with predators of adults were moved to a site whose predator

17
00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:12.797
mostly eats juveniles.

18
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:17.397
The moved population then evolved in its new reach.

19
00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:23.757
David Reznick, Heather Bryga and John Endler compared it with its source population

20
00:01:23.757 --> 00:01:25.037
11 years later.

21
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.907
Notice what is different now.

22
00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:32.427
The moved guppies and their source started as one population.

23
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.807
The researchers made the change, instead of finding sites that already differed.

24
00:01:38.917 --> 00:01:43.237
In the new site, adults are safer and the young are the ones being eaten.

25
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.197
Should the moved guppies now mature earlier, or later?

26
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:55.783
Later, and at a larger size.

27
00:01:56.923 --> 00:02:01.983
The moved population had changed its life history in the direction life-history

28
00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:05.863
theory predicted. It matched the natural comparison.

29
00:02:07.017 --> 00:02:12.217
A manipulation can rule out some explanations that an observation leaves open.

30
00:02:13.437 --> 00:02:16.057
But this is still one stream and one introduction.

31
00:02:17.457 --> 00:02:19.897
And the new reach differed in more than its predator.

32
00:02:21.157 --> 00:02:25.877
So it showed that the change of setting caused evolution more firmly than it showed

33
00:02:25.877 --> 00:02:26.877
exactly how.

34
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:30.607
So there are three tools, each for its own question.

35
00:02:32.047 --> 00:02:35.527
Observation of natural variation shows an association.

36
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:41.787
An experimental contrast tests a cause, because the cause was changed on purpose.

37
00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:45.127
But nobody can move a past event.

38
00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:47.707
That needs the third tool.

39
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:54.607
Historical inference tests rival explanations against the traces the past has left.

40
00:02:55.927 --> 00:02:57.807
Each tool answers a different question.
